+# Keep pickup reminders on SMS
+
+For whoever runs payments, pantry operations or proposes cutting costs. Pickup reminders go out by SMS, not email,
+because email reminders nearly doubled missed pickups the one time larder tried them.
+
+## Context
+
+SMS reminders cost larder about 40 euros a month, and that bill draws a volunteer to propose email, free to send, in
+its place every so often. larder tried it once: from April to June 2025, pickup reminders went out by email instead
+of SMS. The provider's own report showed 34% of those emails were never opened, and missed pickups rose from 6% to
+13% of pantry orders. A missed pickup is a bag already packed for a member who didn't come; by closing time the fresh
+food in it goes in the bin. Many members share one household phone and rarely check email on it, but see a text.
+larder switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back down to 6% by August. Two forces judge the
+options: the reminder must reach a member who shares a household phone, and it must hold the missed-pickup rate at
+6%, not drift toward 13%.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free to send; lost because a third of reminders went unopened and missed pickups nearly doubled while it
+  ran.
+- **SMS**: chosen.
+
+## Decision
+
+Pickup reminders go out by SMS. It is the one option a member sees on a shared household phone, and the only one
+tried that has held missed pickups at 6% of pantry orders. The 40 euros a month it costs is the price of that
+reliability, not an oversight.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Missed pickups, and the food thrown out with them, stay at 6% of pantry orders instead of drifting toward 13%. larder
+carries the SMS bill, about 40 euros a month, as the cost of that. This is the third time a volunteer has proposed
+switching back to email to save that cost; a future proposal needs a new reason, not the cost alone, since the cost
+was already weighed here.
